You are a judge for the "Flip the Switch" hackathon (WeMakeDevs, DevTools Academy, and Switch by SandboxAQ).
Switch is an open-source orchestration layer that puts AI coding agents (Claude Code, Codex, and others) and people into shared team-chat rooms (Slack, Discord, Mattermost), with rooms, roles, addressing policies, templates, documents, and custom agents that join over an HTTP and server-sent-events protocol.

Official rubric:
- Use of Switch, 30%: is Switch central, and would the project stop working without it?
- Technical execution, 25%: does it run, and could someone clone the repo and reproduce it?
- Creativity and impact, 25%: is it a fresh idea that solves a problem a real team has?
- Presentation and documentation, 20%: do the demo video and README make clear what was built and how Switch is used?

The panel includes Switch engineers at SandboxAQ (a security company), DevTools Academy, and WeMakeDevs.
They reward Switch being load-bearing, honest claims, reproducibility, and safe agent behaviour; they distrust staged demos and ideas that a plain bot or CI job could do.
Hundreds of entries are expected; AI code review bots are a common category.

Read the submission plan below (it is a plan; nothing is built yet).
Then:
1. Score each criterion 1-10 with a one-line reason; compute the weighted total out of 10.
2. Say whether you would put it on the shortlist, yes or no, and why in one sentence.
3. List the three weaknesses that cost the most points, most costly first, each with the concrete change that would fix it.
4. Name any claim you doubt and why.
Be strict and specific. Plain text, under 400 words.

Submission plan:
# Ownership Gate

The reviewing team questions the author's coding agent about a pull request; the agent answers from the author's private session with cited steps, the author approves every disclosure, and the reviewer is paged only when nothing is left unexplained.

Track 01, Business workflow. Built on Switch (rooms, roles, addressing, templates, custom protocol agents).

## Problem

Coding agents made pull requests cheap to write and expensive to review, and reviewers cannot tell whether the author understands the change.

| Evidence | Source |
|---|---|
| "Today I announced that I won't be reviewing AI generated PRs at company meeting": 1,560 points, 406 comments, 27 May 2026; data scientists sent Claude Code PRs to the web team | [r/ExperiencedDevs](https://www.reddit.com/r/ExperiencedDevs/comments/1towli9/) |
| "There is an AI code review bubble": 351 points, 249 comments, Jan 2026 | [HN 46766961](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=46766961) |
| Godot maintainers drowning in AI-generated PRs, Mar 2026 | [HN 47251385](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47251385) |
| The Switch repo itself received 155 pull requests between 1 and 4 Nov 2026 | `gh pr list -R sandbox-quantum/switch --state all` |

The hardest case is a cross-team pull request: the author's team wrote it with an agent, the owning team must review it, and the context that explains the change lives only in the author's agent session.

## What exists, and what is still unsolved

| Need | Existing tools | Gap |
|---|---|---|
| Check an agent's claims against its transcript | claimcheck, groundtruth, agentvow, workproof | Run by the author for the author; the reviewer cannot ask anything |
| Make the author answer questions | PR Quiz, PR Guard, Cognit, Gater, sphinx-ci | Questions come from the diff alone, not from what the reviewing team needs to know |
| Show the session to the reviewer | Entire, Copilot coding agent session logs | The whole transcript is pushed; Entire ships `--skip-push-sessions` and redaction because authors do not want that |
| Gate AI pull requests | mergewarden (formerly Agent Gate), CodeRabbit, Copilot review | Judge the diff, not the author's account of it |

**Unsolved:** the reviewing team asks specific questions and gets answers drawn from the author's private session, with citations, without either side handing over its private material.
That needs two owners, two agents (possibly from different vendors), private context on one side, and a live conversation: the shape Switch is built for.

## How it works

1. A watcher (deterministic Switch protocol agent) sees a cross-team pull request and runs a Switch template that opens `#pr-<n>` with the author, the author's agent, and the reviewing team's agent; the human reviewer is not paged yet.
2. A checker (deterministic) posts facts: CI status for the head commit, files changed, tests touched, and transcript claim checks from an existing checker such as claimcheck run on the author's machine.
3. The reviewer's agent asks at most five questions, each tied to a fact or a changed file.
4. The author's agent answers each from the private session and cites the step; the author previews and approves each answer before it posts. Only the author's account can approve; Switch addressing stops anyone else driving the author's agent.
5. Contradictions between the author's answer and the session are posted with the cited step.
6. When every question is answered and no contradiction is open, the reviewer gets one screen: facts, questions, cited answers. Only the reviewer's decision reaches GitHub.
7. Every disclosure and decision is appended to the room's ledger document.

Example:

```
#pr-142  (web team reviewing a pull request from the data team)
reviewer-agent:  Why does this PR change auth/session.py? The linked issue is about CSV export.
author-agent:    Session step 23: the export test failed on an expired session, so the agent
                 raised the session TTL. [author approved this answer]
reviewer:        That is a security change inside a CSV pull request. Split it out.
```

## Why it needs Switch

| Switch feature | Use | Without Switch |
|---|---|---|
| Agents from different owners in one room | Author's agent and reviewing team's agent | No shared place where both agents and both humans meet |
| Addressing policy | Only the author can drive the author's agent | Custom permission code |
| Templates and runs | One room per pull request, tracked as a run | Manual channel setup |
| Custom protocol agents | Watcher, checker, gate | A bot per chat platform |
| Room documents | Ledger of disclosures and decisions | A separate database |
| Any chat app | The same flow on Slack, Discord, or Mattermost | Rebuild per platform |

## Fits Switch Console and the Switch ecosystem

- **Install from Console:** the repo ships `ownership-gate.template.yaml`; a team imports it under Console > Templates and clicks Use template to get the rooms, roles, and agent definitions.
- **Agents from Console providers:** the author's agent is a Claude Code or Codex session Console already runs on the author's machine (or a remote host); nothing new to install for the agent side.
- **Two teams through workspace invites:** the reviewing team joins the author's Switch workspace through Console's Invite people flow; each person keeps ownership of their own agent.
- **GitHub through Switch Connectors:** the watcher and gate use the workspace's GitHub connection rather than a personal token.
- **Approval before disclosure:** the author's agent reading the session is gated by Switch's in-chat approval for tool calls; each answer is previewed before it posts.
- **Secrets stay out:** answers pass through Switch Trust guardrails, when the workspace enables them, before they are stored.
- **Protocol agents:** watcher, checker, and gate register with `register-known` and speak the documented agent protocol, the same path as Switch's own custom-agent examples.

## What is deterministic

Watcher, checker, gate, and ledger are plain Python with no LLM calls.
LLMs only ask questions (reviewer side) and draft answers from the session (author side); the author approves every answer and the gate, not an LLM, decides when the reviewer is paged.

## Impact we will measure

On a fixture set of pull requests written by a teammate who did not see the checker (answer key committed as a hash first):
- reviewer minutes per pull request with and without the gate;
- hidden changes surfaced before review;
- false flags on honest pull requests (target: zero);
- author minutes spent answering.
Numbers are from fixtures, labelled as such; we do not claim real-team savings.

## Reproduce

A replay mode plays recorded events and agent outputs into a fresh local Switch room with no chat workspace or vendor keys, and a solo mode creates the second owner on a local server so one person can run the live flow.

## Limits

- An author can paste questions into another AI; answers are checked against the session, not against who typed them.
- Work done outside the agent is invisible; the reviewer sees "no session evidence" for it.
- Authors who do not run a coding agent get facts and questions only.

